{"purpose":"Define voice capability in two stages, so that the useful part ships early and the part with genuine technical risk is not allowed to block it.","content":"## Stage 1 — asynchronous voice note (in the MVP)\n\nA user captures a short spoken message, which is Opus-encoded and delivered to the recipient through the coordinator like an attachment.\n\n- Interaction is a **toggle**: tap to start capture, tap to send. Hold-to-talk is not portably implementable in a terminal and is offered only as an opt-in enhancement where the Kitty keyboard protocol is detected — see the audio constraints finding.\n- **No latency requirement applies.** There is no jitter buffer, no loss concealment and no real-time path, which is precisely why this stage carries little risk.\n\n## Stage 2 — real-time one-to-one call (after the MVP)\n\nA user calls another device and holds a live conversation, with call notification and visible call state.\n\n- **Latency target: under 100 ms mouth-to-ear on a direct path.** On a relayed path the target does not apply and the requirement is best-effort.\n- The client must **display the active path — direct or relay — and the measured latency**, so that the distinction is visible to the user rather than hidden. An unenforceable blanket target is replaced by a conditional target plus an observable measurement.\n\n## Capability is per-device, not per-deployment\n\nBoth stages live behind the audio build tag. A device built without it must remain completely functional for text, presence and files, and must degrade legibly: it can receive a voice note and be told it cannot play it, rather than failing or hiding the message.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Stage 1: a captured voice note reaches the recipient and plays back intelligibly, on both amd64 and arm64.\n2. Stage 1: a recipient on a build without audio support receives a legible indication that a voice note arrived and cannot be played locally.\n3. Stage 2: measured mouth-to-ear latency on a direct path is under 100 ms on the team's own devices — measured, not calculated.\n4. Stage 2: the client displays whether the active call path is direct or relayed, and its measured latency."}